/-!
# MAIS-O33: no positive corruption fraction is uniformly tolerable

The witness `Causal.Corruption`'s indistinguishability theorem consumes. The
mathematics is MAIS issue [#9](https://github.com/lionellevine/MAIS/issues/9)'s
— Svyatoslav Novikov (kumino), generated by OpenAI Codex, 2026-08-04, CC BY 4.0
— run at `δ = 1/2` rather than the `δ = 0` the note chose, and on
action-independent kernels rather than the note's one skewed row. Both changes
are recorded in `docs/provenance/mais-o33-statability.md`; both are admissible
instances of `prob:corruption`, and both remove a dependency.

## The instance

`𝐒 = Fin (m+2)`, `𝐀 = Fin 2`, `n = 101`, `δ = 1/2`. Then `|𝐀| ≥ 2`, `n > 1`,
and `(n−1)(1−δ) = 50 > 4`, so this is one of the instances print quantifies
over, and a single one suffices: *uniform* tolerability is a claim about every
instance at once.

## Why the kernels ignore the action

`env` puts mass `p` on state `0` and spreads `1−p` over the rest, whatever the
action. That is print's own action-independent class — the one its myopic
converse is stated over — and nothing in `prob:corruption` excludes it. It buys
one thing: `Causal.trajectoryLaw_congr` makes the trajectory law *the same
measure* for every policy, so a `(δ,n)`-bounded agent can be assembled by
choosing a near-optimal policy per (start state, goal) pair and splicing,
without a congruence lemma for `ProbabilityTheory.Kernel.traj` that Mathlib does
not state.

It is not a weakening of the problem. `thm:rabe` claims a `(δ,n)`-bounded agent
determines the kernel to within `√(2/50) ≈ 0.2` on *any* communicating instance,
these two are `0.8` apart, and the agents built here agree except on the
exceptional goals. So the informative pairs are exactly that doubly
exponentially small set — which is the whole point of `prob:corruption`, not an
artefact of the class.

## Why `δ = 1/2` and not print's `δ = 0`

At `δ = 0` the boundedness clause reads `P(τ ⊨ ψ | π, s₀) = ⨆_{π'} …`, so the
two agents would have to be *exactly* optimal at every pair, and the existence
of optimal policies for reachability objectives on a finite controlled Markov
process becomes unavoidable. Mathlib does not carry it. At any `δ > 0` ordinary
supremum approximation suffices (`Causal.exists_achieveProb_ge`), and
`δ = 1/2` still leaves `(n−1)(1−δ) = 50`, which both clears print's constraint
and keeps the two worlds outside each other's reconstruction balls.

Nothing here uses `sorry` or an added axiom.
-/

namespace AISafetyAtlas.Examples.Causal.O33Corruption

open AISafetyAtlas.Causal Finset
open scoped ENNReal

/-! ## The two environments -/

/-- The exogenous chain that puts mass `p` on state `0` and spreads the rest
uniformly. The action is never read, which is print's own action-independent
class.-/
@[expose] public noncomputable def env (m : ℕ) {p : ℝ} (hp0 : 0 ≤ p) (hp1 : p ≤ 1) :
    ControlledMarkovProcess (Fin (m + 2)) (Fin 2) where
  prob := fun _ _ s' ↦ if s' = 0 then p else (1 - p) / (m + 1)
  prob_nonneg := by
    intro s a s'
    by_cases h : s' = 0
    · simpa [h] using hp0
    · have : (0 : ℝ) < (m : ℝ) + 1 := by positivity
      simp only [h, ite_false]
      exact div_nonneg (by linarith) this.le
  prob_sum := by
    intro s a
    classical
    rw [← Finset.sum_erase_add _ _ (Finset.mem_univ (0 : Fin (m + 2)))]
    have hrest : ∀ x ∈ (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin (m + 2))).erase 0,
        (if x = 0 then p else (1 - p) / (m + 1)) = (1 - p) / (m + 1) := fun x hx ↦
      ite_eq_right (Finset.ne_of_mem_erase hx)
    rw [Finset.sum_congr rfl hrest, Finset.sum_const,
      Finset.card_erase_of_mem (Finset.mem_univ _), Finset.card_univ, Fintype.card_fin,
      ite_eq_left rfl, nsmul_eq_mul]
    have hm : (0 : ℝ) < (m : ℝ) + 1 := by positivity
    have hcast : ((m + 2 - 1 : ℕ) : ℝ) = (m : ℝ) + 1 := by
      push_cast [Nat.add_sub_cancel]
      ring
    rw [hcast]
    field_simp
    ring

/-- The world that stays at state `0` nine times in ten.-/
@[expose] public noncomputable def envHi (m : ℕ) :
    ControlledMarkovProcess (Fin (m + 2)) (Fin 2) :=
  env m (p := 9 / 10) (by norm_num) (by norm_num)

/-- The world that leaves state `0` nine times in ten.-/
@[expose] public noncomputable def envLo (m : ℕ) :
    ControlledMarkovProcess (Fin (m + 2)) (Fin 2) :=
  env m (p := 1 / 10) (by norm_num) (by norm_num)

/-! ## The instance

`n = 101` and `δ = 1/2` clear print's `(n−1)(1−δ) > 4` with room: the product is
`50`, so the target radius is `2/√50` and the two worlds are `4/5` apart, which
is more than twice it. Print's `δ = 0` would force exact optimality on the two
agents and with it the optimal-policy-attainment theorem; `δ = 1/2` is
admissible and needs only supremum approximation. -/

/-- Print's depth for this instance.-/
@[expose] public def o33Depth : ℕ := 101

/-- Print's failure rate for this instance.-/
@[expose] public noncomputable def o33Delta : ℝ := 1 / 2

/-! ## The two agents, and the one corruption that serves both -/

end AISafetyAtlas.Examples.Causal.O33Corruption
